.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt GETTIMEOFDAY 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gettimeofday ,
.Nm clock_gettime ,
.Nm times ,
.Nm utimensat
.Nd read clocks and set file timestamps
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/time.h
.Ft int
.Fn gettimeofday "struct timeval *restrict tv" "void *restrict tz"
.In time.h
.Ft int
.Fn clock_gettime "clockid_t clockid" "struct timespec *tp"
.In sys/times.h
.Ft clock_t
.Fn times "struct tms *buf"
.In sys/stat.h
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn utimensat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "const struct timespec times[2]" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn gettimeofday
reports real time in
.Fa tv ,
as seconds and microseconds.
The
.Fa tz
argument is ignored.
Real time is the CMOS clock reading taken at boot plus elapsed timer ticks.
If the clock could not be read, the result is time since boot.
The hardware clock is read once; no service corrects the clock afterward.
.Pp
.Fn clock_gettime
stores seconds and nanoseconds in
.Fa tp .
Only two clock IDs are supported:
.Bl -tag -width "CLOCK_MONOTONIC"
.It Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
ID 0.
The same real time reported by
.Fn gettimeofday .
.It Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
ID 1.
Time since boot, computed from the kernel's 32-bit tick counter, which
wraps after about 497 days.
.El
.Pp
Both clocks have 10 ms resolution.
The PIT microsecond clock used for kernel diagnostics reads the counter
within a tick; these system calls do not expose that finer clock.
.Pp
.Fn times
returns the monotonic tick count in units of 1/100 second, as a signed
32-bit value that goes negative after about 249 days.
Per-process CPU time is not tracked.
If
.Fa buf
is nonnull, all four fields,
.Va tms_utime ,
.Va tms_stime ,
.Va tms_cutime ,
and
.Va tms_cstime ,
are set to zero.
A null
.Fa buf
is allowed.
.Ss File timestamps
.Fn utimensat
sets the access and modification times of
.Fa path .
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
A relative path is resolved against the current working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD ,
or against the directory referred to by
.Fa dirfd
otherwise.
.Pp
The
.Fa times
argument points to two timespecs: access time first, modification time second.
A null pointer sets both to the current time.
For either element, a
.Va tv_nsec
of
.Dv UTIME_NOW
selects the current time;
.Dv UTIME_OMIT
leaves that timestamp unchanged.
When both elements specify
.Dv UTIME_OMIT ,
neither timestamp nor the change time is changed.
.Pp
Setting both timestamps to the current time requires ownership, write
permission on the file, or effective user ID 0.
Any other change requires ownership or effective user ID 0.
Omitting both timestamps requires neither, but the path must still resolve.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
argument is 0 or
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW .
The latter changes a symbolic link's own timestamps instead of following it.
Other bits are rejected.
The kernel does not range-check explicit nanosecond values.
.Pp
Timestamp changes are supported on KRFFS, ext2, FAT32, and exFAT,
subject to the file system's stored precision.
On KRFFS, FAT32, and exFAT, a successful request on the volume's root
directory leaves its timestamps unchanged.
The root directory of the KRFFS boot file system accepts these requests
without checking ownership; its other nodes undergo the checks above.
Synthetic files under
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
return
.Er EROFS ,
including when both timestamps are omitted.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 78 for
.Fn gettimeofday ,
265 for
.Fn clock_gettime ,
43 for
.Fn times ,
and 320 for
.Fn utimensat .
The time structures in this kernel interface have 32-bit seconds fields.
The signed 32-bit seconds field overflows in 2038.
.Pp
musl first tries
.Fn clock_gettime64
.Pq system call 403 ,
which the kernel does not implement, then converts the result of the
32-bit call into its public timespec.
musl implements
.Fn gettimeofday
through
.Fn clock_gettime ;
it accepts a null
.Fa tv
and returns 0.
The raw
.Fn gettimeofday
system call requires a writable, nonnull timeval.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn gettimeofday ,
.Fn clock_gettime ,
and
.Fn utimensat
return 0 on success.
.Fn times
returns the tick count.
Kernel failures return a negative error number; the system call wrappers
return -1 and set
.Va errno ,
except musl's
.Fn times ,
which returns the kernel's result as it is: an invalid buffer gives the
raw -14 of
.Er EFAULT .
musl's
.Fn gettimeofday
returns 0 and writes its result in user space.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/timeprobe.c
reads
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
twice and checks that the second result is not earlier than the first.
.Pa tests/miscprobe.c
checks that
.Fn times
sets the four process-time fields to zero.
.Sh ERRORS
The kernel handlers return the following errors:
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
For
.Fn utimensat ,
an ancestor directory denies search permission, or a caller that is
neither the owner nor privileged tries to set both times to the current
time without write permission.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fn utimensat
was given an invalid directory descriptor for a relative path, or the
descriptor refers to a removed KRFFS boot-root directory.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The
.Fn utimensat
path enters a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A required buffer cannot be read or written.
The raw
.Fn gettimeofday
timeval or
.Fn clock_gettime
timespec is null.
For
.Fn utimensat ,
the path is null or there is no current process.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa clockid
is neither
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
nor
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
or
.Fn utimensat
has flag bits other than
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW .
.It Bq Er EIO
For
.Fn utimensat ,
the backing device has been removed, or a file-system read or metadata
write fails.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
The
.Fn utimensat
path requires more than 40 symbolic links to be followed.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The
.Fn utimensat
path, a component, a reconstructed directory path, or an expanded
symbolic-link path exceeds the resolver's buffer limit.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The
.Fn utimensat
path is empty or a component does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The file system cannot allocate a node while resolving the
.Fn utimensat
path.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A component of the
.Fn utimensat
path is not a directory, or a descriptor used as the base of a relative
path is not a directory.
.It Bq Er EPERM
A caller that is neither the owner nor privileged requests a timestamp
change other than setting both times to the current time.
.It Bq Er EROFS
.Fn utimensat
targets a synthetic file, or a timestamp update reaches a read-only
mount or a file system without a timestamp-setting operation.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
.Xr stat 2
